Due to the formation of this very stable complex, the concentration of free \(Cu^{+}\) ions in the solution becomes extremely low. Therefore, when \(H_{2}S(g)\) is passed through this solution, the ionic product \(Q_{sp}\) for \(CuS\) (or \(Cu_{2}S\)) remains less than its solubility product \(K_{sp}\). As a result, no \(CuS\) precipitate (metal sulphide) is formed.
