\text{Coefficient of } x^{48} \text{ in } S = - {}^{101}C_{50} + 100 \cdot {}^{101}C_{49}
Rearranging the terms:
\text{Coefficient of } x^{48} \text{ in } S = 100 \cdot {}^{101}C_{49} - {}^{101}C_{50}

Let the given integral be \(I\).
I = \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2} \frac{1}{[x]+4}dx
We know that \(-\pi/2 \approx -1.57\) and \(\pi/2 \approx 1.57\).
The greatest integer function \([x]\) changes its value at integer points. We need to split the integral based on the values of \([x]\) in the interval \([-\pi/2, \pi/2]\).
• For \(x \in [-\pi/2, -1)\), \([x] = -2\)
• For \(x \in [-1, 0)\), \([x] = -1\)
• For \(x \in [0, 1)\), \([x] = 0\)
• For \(x \in [1, \pi/2)\), \([x] = 1\)
So, we can rewrite the integral as:
I = \int_{-\pi/2}^{-1} \frac{dx}{-2+4} + \int_{-1}^{0} \frac{dx}{-1+4} + \int_{0}^{1} \frac{dx}{0+4} + \int_{1}^{\pi/2} \frac{dx}{1+4}
I = \int_{-\pi/2}^{-1} \frac{dx}{2} + \int_{-1}^{0} \frac{dx}{3} + \int_{0}^{1} \frac{dx}{4} + \int_{1}^{\pi/2} \frac{dx}{5}
Now, evaluate each definite integral:
I = \frac{1}{2}[x]_{-\pi/2}^{-1} + \frac{1}{3}[x]_{-1}^{0} + \frac{1}{4}[x]_{0}^{1} + \frac{1}{5}[x]_{1}^{\pi/2}
I = \frac{1}{2}(-1 - (-\pi/2)) + \frac{1}{3}(0 - (-1)) + \frac{1}{4}(1 - 0) + \frac{1}{5}(\pi/2 - 1)
I = \frac{1}{2}(-1 + \frac{\pi}{2}) + \frac{1}{3}(1) + \frac{1}{4}(1) + \frac{1}{5}(\frac{\pi}{2} - 1)
I = -\frac{1}{2} + \frac{\pi}{4} + \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{4} + \frac{\pi}{10} - \frac{1}{5}
Group the terms with \(\pi\) and constant terms:
I = \left(\frac{\pi}{4} + \frac{\pi}{10}\right) + \left(-\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{5}\right)
For the \(\pi\) terms, the common denominator is 20:
\frac{5\pi}{20} + \frac{2\pi}{20} = \frac{7\pi}{20}
For the constant terms, the common denominator is 60:
\frac{-30}{60} + \frac{20}{60} + \frac{15}{60} - \frac{12}{60} = \frac{-30+20+15-12}{60} = \frac{-7}{60}
Combine these results:
I = \frac{7\pi}{20} - \frac{7}{60}
To simplify, find a common denominator (60):
I = \frac{3 \times 7\pi}{3 \times 20} - \frac{7}{60} = \frac{21\pi}{60} - \frac{7}{60}
I = \frac{21\pi - 7}{60}
Factor out 7 from the numerator:
I = \frac{7(3\pi - 1)}{60}
This matches option (3).

